  "verbalization": "Gomisin C is shown to have a direct target relationship with Jurkat through multiple pathways, all of which converge on the enzyme endothelial lipase. The evidence suggests that Gomisin C inhibits endothelial lipase, which is a key enzyme involved in lipid metabolism and has been implicated in various pathological processes. The target of endothelial lipase is consistently identified as (4aR,8aS)-4a,7,7-trimethyl-3,4,5,6,8,8a-hexahydro-1H-naphthalen-2-one, and in another pathway, the target is identified as 1-[(2S,3S)-2-(3-Hydroxyprop-1-En-2-Yl)-3-[(2R,3R,4S,5S,6R)-3,4,5-Trihydroxy-6-(Hydroxymethyl)Oxan-2-Yl]Oxy-2,3-Dihydro-1-Benzofuran-5-Yl]Ethanone. These compounds are all linked to Jurkat, a cell line often used in cancer research, suggesting a potential role in modulating cellular processes in this context.\n\nThe mechanism of action appears to involve the inhibition of endothelial lipase, which may influence lipid homeostasis and downstream signaling pathways. The consistent presence of Jurkat as a target across different pathways supports a potential therapeutic role for Gomisin C in targeting this enzyme. However, the exact biological relevance of this relationship remains to be fully elucidated, and further experimental validation is needed to confirm the functional impact of Gomisin C on Jurkat cells.",
